# The synthetic-market generator of the stress test. Students do not edit this file.
#
# The stress test runs the fund on synthetic markets that move together like the real market. The instructor fit
# the general factor model to the real growth rates of 2016-01-04 to 2024-12-31 and stored the fit in
# data/generator.jld2. The model explains the growth rate of each ticker with three parts: the market (the growth
# rate of SPY), the 11 GICS sector factors, and the ticker's own part:
#
#   g_i(t) = α_i + β_i(t) g_m(t) + Σ_s δ_is f_s(t) + ψ_i u_i(t)
#
# The market and the sector factors are correlated: Real Estate and Utilities, for example, tend to rise and
# fall together. The fit untangles the 12 factors into 12 uncorrelated series with the Cholesky factor L of
# their covariance matrix (the Cholesky step), and it fits one jump hidden Markov model to each uncorrelated
# series and one to the own part of each ticker. To draw a synthetic market, we draw each series on its own,
# mix the uncorrelated draws back into correlated factors with L, and compose the growth rate of each ticker.
# The fit loads every ticker on all 11 sector factors, ETFs included. The stress test grows SGOV at the T-bill
# rate and holds USDG at its peg outside a depeg. SPY is the market itself, so it has α = 0, β = 1, no sector
# loadings, and no own part.
#
# The stress test moves the stock intercepts α toward their average and gives the stock market sensitivities β
# a drift around their fitted values. The drift has a 126-trading-day half-life and a scale from data/beta-drift.csv.
# The own scale ψ is reduced to account for the variance of the drift. The ETFs keep their fitted parameters.
#
# The generator file holds the series for each model, which take about a fifth of the space of the fitted models.
# load_generator fits the models from the stored series and settings. Fitting draws no random numbers.
#
# The file reads from top to bottom:
#   1. fit_jump_model    fits one jump hidden Markov model to one series
#   2. load_beta_scales  reads the stock drift scales and assigns their median to a stock without a scale
#   3. load_generator    reads the generator file, fits the models, and sets the stock intercepts and own scales
#   4. draw_series       draws one path of a series from its jump hidden Markov model
#   5. synthetic_growth  draws one synthetic market: the growth rate of every ticker on every day
#
# Names used in this file, with their units:
#   tickers        Vector{String}   the tickers of the generator: every ticker of the prices file except SGOV
#   sector         Vector{String}   the GICS sector of each ticker, or "ETF"
#   sector_names   Vector{String}   the 11 GICS sectors, in the order of the rows of Δ and of the sector factors
#   α              Vector{Float64}  growth-rate intercept of each ticker after the pull toward the mean (1/yr)
#   β              Vector{Float64}  fitted market sensitivity of each ticker (dimensionless)
#   β_halflife     Float64          half-life of the stock β drift (trading days)
#   β_scale        Vector{Float64}  scale of the β drift for each ticker (dimensionless), zero for ETFs
#   Δ              Matrix{Float64}  sector loadings, sectors × tickers: column i holds the 11 loadings of ticker i
#   ψ              Vector{Float64}  scale of the own part of each ticker after accounting for the β drift
#   μ              Vector{Float64}  the average of each factor (1/yr): the market first, then the 11 sector factors
#   chol_L         Matrix{Float64}  the lower Cholesky factor L of the covariance matrix of the factors, 12 × 12 (1/yr)
#   Z              Matrix{Float64}  the 12 uncorrelated series of the fit window, days × 12: the market first, then
#                                   one column per sector (dimensionless, variance 1)
#   U              Matrix{Float64}  the own part of each ticker in the fit window, days × tickers (1/yr). A day
#                                   without a growth rate of the ticker is NaN, and the column of SPY is all NaN
#   number_of_states  Int           the number of hidden states of each model, 100
#   ν              Float64          degrees of freedom of the Student-t distribution of each hidden state, 5.0
#   ϵ              Float64          chance per day that a jump episode starts, tuned on the market series
#   λ              Float64          mean length of a jump episode (trading days), tuned on the market series
#   Δt             Float64          one trading day, 1/252 (yr)
#   market_model   JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel
#                                   the jump hidden Markov model of the market series, column 1 of Z
#   factor_models  Vector{JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel}
#                                   the 11 jump hidden Markov models of the sector series, columns 2 to 12 of Z
#   own_models     Dict{String,JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel}
#                                   the jump hidden Markov model of the own part of each ticker, by ticker (SPY has none)
#   start_close    Vector{Float64}  the close of each ticker on start_date (USD per share)
#   start_date     Date             the last day of the stored prices, where the synthetic paths start
#   fit_start      Date             the first day of the fit window, 2016-01-04
#   fit_stop       Date             the last day of the fit window, 2024-12-31
#   days           Int              the length of a synthetic path (trading days)
#   seed           Int              the seed of the random numbers
#   g              Float64          growth rate, g = (1/Δt) ln(S(t+Δt)/S(t)) with Δt = 1/252 (1/yr)
#
# Julia notes for this file:
#   import JumpHMM           makes the package available; its functions are called with its name, JumpHMM.simulate(...)
#   f(a; b = 1)              the arguments after the semicolon are keyword arguments, passed by name, such as
#                            JumpHMM.simulate(model, days; number_of_paths = 1)
#   isfile(path)             is true when a file exists at path
#   jldopen(path, "r")       opens a JLD2 file; file["name"] reads the entry called name; close(file) closes it
#   (a = 1, b = 2)           a NamedTuple: a group of values that you read by name, such as generator.α;
#                            return (name = value, ...) returns one
#   Dict{String,T}()         an empty dictionary from text to values of type T; dict[key] = value adds an entry
#   haskey(dict, key)        is true when the dictionary dict has an entry for key
#   push!(list, x)           adds x at the end of list
#   isfinite(x)              is true when x is a number that is not NaN and not infinite
#   Random.seed!(seed)       fixes the random numbers, so that every run with the same seed draws the same values
#   Random.Xoshiro(seed)     creates a separate random number generator
#   Int128(n)                stores an integer with room for the offset added to the drift seed
#   Random.randn(rng, n, m)  draws an n × m matrix of standard normal values from rng
#   zeros(n, m)              an n × m matrix of zeros; zeros(n) is a list of n zeros
#   Z[:, j]                  column j of Z, every row; Y[:, 2:end] keeps the columns from 2 to the last
#   transpose(A)             the transpose of A; A * B multiplies two matrices
#   transpose(μ) .+ M        adds the list μ to every row of the matrix M; the dot repeats the addition
#   a .* x                   multiplies every entry of x by a
#   a || b                   is true when a is true, or b is true, or both; !a is true when a is false
#   size(A)                  the numbers of rows and columns of A, such as (11, 506)
#   LinearAlgebra.istril(A)  is true when every entry of A above the diagonal is zero
#   throw(ArgumentError(text))   stops the program with an error message

# JumpHMM fits and draws the models, CSV reads the drift table, Random supplies the random draws,
# and LinearAlgebra checks the Cholesky factor -
import CSV, JumpHMM, LinearAlgebra, Random;
using JLD2;
using Statistics: mean, median, var;

"""
    fit_jump_model(series::Vector{Float64}, number_of_states::Int, ν::Float64, ϵ::Float64, λ::Float64,
        Δt::Float64) -> JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel

Fit a single-asset jump hidden Markov model to one series.

### Arguments

- `series`: The series, one value per day: an uncorrelated series of the factors, or the own part of a ticker.
- `number_of_states`: The number of hidden states.
- `ν`: The degrees of freedom of the Student-t distribution of each hidden state.
- `ϵ`: The chance per day that a jump episode starts.
- `λ`: The mean length of a jump episode (trading days).
- `Δt`: One trading day (yr).

### Returns

- The fitted jump hidden Markov model of the series.

### Method

`JumpHMM.build` cuts the series into `number_of_states` hidden states, counts the moves between the states from one day to the next, and fits a Student-t distribution to the values of each state. The fit draws no random numbers, so the same series and settings always give the same model.

### Course references

Fitting a single-asset jump hidden Markov model, from the lectures on synthetic markets.
"""
function fit_jump_model(series::Vector{Float64}, number_of_states::Int, ν::Float64, ϵ::Float64, λ::Float64,
    Δt::Float64)::JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel

    # JumpHMM.build takes the type of the model to build and a NamedTuple of its inputs -
    return JumpHMM.build(JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel, (growth_rates = series,
        number_of_states = number_of_states, ν = ν, ϵ = ϵ, λ = λ, Δt = Δt));
end

"""
    load_beta_scales(tickers::Vector{String}, sector::Vector{String}) -> Vector{Float64}

Read the market-sensitivity drift scales for the stocks.

### Arguments

- `tickers`: The tickers in generator order.
- `sector`: The sector label of each ticker, with "ETF" for exchange-traded funds.

### Returns

- One drift scale per ticker. A stock missing from the table gets the median scale, and an ETF gets zero.

### Method

Read `data/beta-drift.csv` from the checkout that supplies this source file, skipping its header line that starts with
`#`. Use its scale for each matching stock.
Use the median of the full table for each remaining stock.

### Course references

The stock market sensitivities in the synthetic markets.
"""
function load_beta_scales(tickers::Vector{String}, sector::Vector{String})::Vector{Float64}

    # Read the fixed calibration table next to the source, even when the generator file is in another folder -
    path = joinpath(@__DIR__, "..", "data", "beta-drift.csv");
    rows = CSV.File(path; comment = "#", types = Dict(:ticker => String, :k_beta => Float64), strict = true);
    if isempty(rows)
        throw(ArgumentError("The stock drift scale table is empty."));
    end

    # Store the calibrated scales by ticker and take their median for stocks missing from the table -
    scale_by_ticker = Dict{String,Float64}();
    for k ∈ 1:length(rows)
        ticker = rows[k].ticker;
        scale = rows[k].k_beta;
        if ismissing(ticker) || ismissing(scale) || !isfinite(scale) || scale < 0.0
            throw(ArgumentError("Each row of the stock drift scale table needs a ticker and a nonnegative finite scale."));
        end
        if haskey(scale_by_ticker, ticker)
            throw(ArgumentError("The stock drift scale table lists $(ticker) more than once."));
        end
        scale_by_ticker[ticker] = scale;
    end
    median_scale = median(collect(values(scale_by_ticker)));

    # The table calibrates stocks, so ETFs keep their fitted market sensitivities -
    β_scale = zeros(length(tickers));
    for i ∈ 1:length(tickers)
        if sector[i] != "ETF"
            β_scale[i] = get(scale_by_ticker, tickers[i], median_scale);
        end
    end
    return β_scale;
end

"""
    load_generator(path::String) -> NamedTuple

Read the synthetic-market generator from the generator file, and fit its jump hidden Markov models again.

### Arguments

- `path`: The path of the generator file, such as `data/generator.jld2`.

### Returns

- A `NamedTuple` with the fields `tickers`, `sector`, `sector_names`, `α`, `β`, `β_halflife`, `β_scale`, `Δ`, `ψ`, `μ`, `chol_L`, `market_model`, `factor_models`, `own_models`, `start_close`, `start_date`, `fit_start`, and `fit_stop`. The names are listed in the header of this file. `fit_start` and `fit_stop` are the first and the last day of the fit window.

### Errors

- An `ArgumentError` when the file does not exist, or when the sizes of the entries do not agree with each other.

### Method

Open the file, read each entry by name, and check that the sizes agree: one α, β, ψ, sector, close, and column of `U` per ticker, one column of `Δ` per ticker and one row per sector, and one entry of `μ`, one row and column of `chol_L`, and one column of `Z` per factor (the market and the sectors). Then fit the models with `fit_jump_model` and the stored settings: the market model to column 1 of `Z`, one sector model to each of columns 2 to 12, and one own model to the finite values of each column of `U`. SPY has no own part, so it gets no own model.

Pull each stock's intercept toward the average stock intercept, keeping 62% of the original difference. Give each stock's β a 126-trading-day half-life and its calibrated drift scale. Multiplying the stationary variance of the β drift by the market growth-rate variance gives the variance added by the drift. Subtract that amount from the variance assigned to the stock's own term before computing its scale. The ETFs keep their fitted intercepts and own scales.

### Course references

The general factor model with its fitting algorithm and the Cholesky step, and fitting a single-asset jump hidden Markov model, from the lectures on synthetic markets.
"""
function load_generator(path::String)::NamedTuple

    # The file must exist -
    if !isfile(path)
        throw(ArgumentError("The generator file $(path) does not exist."));
    end

    # Read each entry of the file by name -
    file = jldopen(path, "r");
    tickers = file["tickers"];
    sector = file["sector"];
    sector_names = file["sector_names"];
    α = file["α"];
    β = file["β"];
    Δ = file["Δ"];
    ψ = file["ψ"];
    μ = file["μ"];
    chol_L = file["chol_L"];
    Z = file["Z"];
    U = file["U"];
    number_of_states = file["number_of_states"];
    ν = file["ν"];
    ϵ = file["ϵ"];
    λ = file["λ"];
    Δt = file["Δt"];
    start_close = file["start_close"];
    start_date = file["start_date"];
    fit_start = file["fit_start"];
    fit_stop = file["fit_stop"];
    close(file);

    # Each ticker has one sector, one α, one β, one ψ, one close, one column of Δ, and one column of U -
    number_of_tickers = length(tickers);
    if length(sector) != number_of_tickers || length(α) != number_of_tickers || length(β) != number_of_tickers ||
       length(ψ) != number_of_tickers || length(start_close) != number_of_tickers
        throw(ArgumentError("The generator file holds a different number of values than tickers."));
    end
    if size(Δ) != (length(sector_names), number_of_tickers)
        throw(ArgumentError("The sector loadings Δ must have one row per sector and one column per ticker."));
    end
    if size(U) != (size(Z, 1), number_of_tickers)
        throw(ArgumentError("The own parts U must have one row per day of Z and one column per ticker."));
    end

    # The factors are the market and the sectors. Each factor has one average, one row and one column of the
    # Cholesky factor, and one column of Z -
    number_of_factors = 1 + length(sector_names);
    if length(μ) != number_of_factors || size(chol_L) != (number_of_factors, number_of_factors) ||
       size(Z, 2) != number_of_factors
        throw(ArgumentError("The generator file must hold one average, one Cholesky row, and one series per factor."));
    end
    if !LinearAlgebra.istril(chol_L)
        throw(ArgumentError("The Cholesky factor chol_L must be lower triangular."));
    end

    # Fit the market model to column 1 of Z, and one sector model to each of the columns 2 to 12 -
    market_model = fit_jump_model(Z[:, 1], number_of_states, ν, ϵ, λ, Δt);
    factor_models = JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel[];
    for s ∈ 1:length(sector_names)
        push!(factor_models, fit_jump_model(Z[:, 1 + s], number_of_states, ν, ϵ, λ, Δt));
    end

    # Pull each stock intercept toward the average of the fitted stock intercepts -
    stock_columns = Int[];
    for i ∈ 1:number_of_tickers
        if sector[i] != "ETF"
            push!(stock_columns, i);
        end
    end
    if !isempty(stock_columns)
        α_mean = mean(α[stock_columns]);
        for k ∈ 1:length(stock_columns)
            i = stock_columns[k];
            α[i] = α_mean + 0.62 * (α[i] - α_mean);
        end
    end

    # The first diagonal entry of the Cholesky factor is the standard deviation of the market growth rate -
    β_halflife = 126.0;
    β_scale = load_beta_scales(tickers, sector);
    market_variance = chol_L[1, 1]^2;

    # Fit each own model to the finite values of its column of U, in date order. The SPY column has no
    # finite values, so SPY has no own model -
    own_models = Dict{String,JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel}();
    for i ∈ 1:number_of_tickers
        own_part = Float64[];
        for t ∈ 1:size(U, 1)
            if isfinite(U[t, i])
                push!(own_part, U[t, i]);
            end
        end
        if length(own_part) > 0
            own_models[tickers[i]] = fit_jump_model(own_part, number_of_states, ν, ϵ, λ, Δt);
        end

        # The fitted own part already includes variation from β. Reserve the variance of the synthetic
        # drift before setting the scale of the own draw -
        if β_scale[i] > 0.0
            own_variance = var(own_part);
            if !isfinite(own_variance) || own_variance <= 0.0
                throw(ArgumentError("Stock $(tickers[i]) needs positive own variance for β drift."));
            end
            drift_variance = JumpHMM.drift_variance(β_halflife, β_scale[i]) * market_variance;
            ψ[i] = JumpHMM.own_scale(ψ[i]^2 * own_variance, own_variance, drift_variance; ticker = tickers[i]);
        end
    end

    return (tickers = tickers, sector = sector, sector_names = sector_names, α = α, β = β, β_halflife = β_halflife,
        β_scale = β_scale, Δ = Δ, ψ = ψ, μ = μ, chol_L = chol_L, market_model = market_model,
        factor_models = factor_models, own_models = own_models, start_close = start_close, start_date = start_date,
        fit_start = fit_start, fit_stop = fit_stop);
end

"""
    draw_series(model::JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel, days::Int) -> Vector{Float64}

Draw one path of a series from its jump hidden Markov model.

### Arguments

- `model`: The jump hidden Markov model of the series: the market, a sector, or the own part of a ticker.
- `days`: The length of the path (trading days).

### Returns

- The path, one value per day.

### Method

`JumpHMM.simulate` draws one path with the random numbers of the global generator, so that a call of `Random.seed!` before the draws fixes the path. The result of `JumpHMM.simulate` holds one column per path in its field `growth_rates`, and `[:, 1]` keeps the column of the one path.

### Course references

Simulating a single-asset jump hidden Markov model, from the lectures on synthetic markets.
"""
function draw_series(model::JumpHMM.MyJumpHiddenMarkovModel, days::Int)::Vector{Float64}

    # Draw one path. The function does not reseed, so the path depends on the draws before it -
    return JumpHMM.simulate(model, days; number_of_paths = 1).growth_rates[:, 1];
end

"""
    synthetic_growth(generator::NamedTuple, days::Int, seed::Int) -> Matrix{Float64}

Draw one synthetic market from the generator: the growth rate of every ticker of the generator on every day.

### Arguments

- `generator`: The generator from `load_generator`.
- `days`: The length of the synthetic market (trading days).
- `seed`: The seed of the random numbers. The same seed always draws the same market.

### Returns

- The synthetic growth rates (1/yr), days × tickers: column `i` holds the ticker `generator.tickers[i]`.

### Errors

- An `ArgumentError` when `days` is less than 1.

### Method

Fix the seed, then follow the sampling algorithm of the general factor model. Step 1: draw a path of each uncorrelated series into the columns of `Z_synthetic`, the market model first and then the 11 sector models, and mix the draws back into correlated factors, `Y = 1μᵀ + Z_synthetic Lᵀ`. Column 1 of `Y` is the market `g_m`, and columns 2 to 12 are the sector factors `F`. Step 2: draw the own part `u` of each ticker from its model (SPY has none). Draw the stock β drifts from a separate seeded random number generator. Start each drift in its stationary distribution and update it with an exponential moving average of standard normal shocks. Step 3: compose the growth rate of each ticker, `α + β(t) g_m + F δ + ψ u`. The function reseeds the global random number generator for the jump hidden Markov model draws.

### Course references

The sampling algorithm of the general factor model and the Cholesky step, from the lectures on synthetic markets.
"""
function synthetic_growth(generator::NamedTuple, days::Int, seed::Int)::Matrix{Float64}

    # A synthetic market has at least one day -
    if days < 1
        throw(ArgumentError("A synthetic market needs at least one day."));
    end

    # Fix the seed, so that the same seed always draws the same market. The draws below come one after the
    # other in a fixed order: the market, the 11 sectors, then the own part of each ticker in the order of tickers -
    Random.seed!(seed);

    # Sampling step 1: draw a path of each uncorrelated series. Column 1 of Z_synthetic holds the market draw, and
    # column 1 + s holds the draw of sector s -
    number_of_factors = length(generator.μ);
    Z_synthetic = zeros(days, number_of_factors);
    Z_synthetic[:, 1] = draw_series(generator.market_model, days);
    for s ∈ 1:(number_of_factors - 1)
        Z_synthetic[:, 1 + s] = draw_series(generator.factor_models[s], days);
    end

    # Mix the draws back into correlated factors, Y = 1μᵀ + Z_synthetic Lᵀ. Column 1 of Y is the market g_m, and
    # the other columns are the sector factors F, in the order of sector_names -
    Y = transpose(generator.μ) .+ Z_synthetic * transpose(generator.chol_L);
    g_m = Y[:, 1];
    F = Y[:, 2:end];

    # Draw the shocks of the stock β drift separately so that the factor and own draws keep their sequence -
    number_of_tickers = length(generator.tickers);
    drift_rng = Random.Xoshiro(Int128(3 * 10^12) + seed);
    shocks = Random.randn(drift_rng, days, number_of_tickers);

    # Sampling steps 2 and 3: draw the own part and compose the growth rates from the market, the sector factors,
    # and the own part. SPY is the market, so its own part stays zero -
    G = zeros(days, number_of_tickers);
    for i ∈ 1:number_of_tickers
        ticker = generator.tickers[i];
        u = zeros(days);
        if haskey(generator.own_models, ticker)
            u = draw_series(generator.own_models[ticker], days);
        end
        # Each stock's market sensitivity is its fitted β plus its drift on that day -
        β = generator.β[i];
        if generator.β_scale[i] > 0.0
            drift = JumpHMM.drift_path(shocks[:, i], generator.β_halflife, generator.β_scale[i]);
            β = β .+ drift;
        end

        # Add the market, sector, and own terms to the intercept -
        G[:, i] = generator.α[i] .+ β .* g_m .+ F * generator.Δ[:, i] .+ generator.ψ[i] .* u;
    end
    return G;
end
